{# exportarr sidecar for the *arr stack — sonarr, radarr, prowlarr.

   One macro rather than three near-identical container blocks, for the reason the compose
   templates share `traefik.yml.j2` and `resources.yml.j2`: three copies drift, and the drift
   is invisible until one of them is the only app without a metric someone needs.

   WHY A SIDECAR, not a Deployment of its own. exportarr reaches its *arr over
   `http://localhost:<port>` inside the shared pod network namespace. As a separate workload it
   would need a ClusterIP hop, an entry in that *arr's NetworkPolicy caller list, and a fourth
   thing to keep in step. In-pod it needs none of those — and it is admitted to Prometheus for
   free, because the netpol baseline grants prometheus every pod carrying
   `netpol-baseline: enforced` on ANY port (no `ports:` key on that rule).

   `image` is a PARAMETER, not a literal. `test_no_literal_image_lines_in_k8s_templates` fails a
   hardcoded image in a k8s template. Each *arr pins its own copy in its `defaults/main.yml`
   rather than sharing one var in `k8s/vars.yml`. Renovate does read that file, but its
   no-automerge `k8s_autodeploy: false` rule names that file. All three *arr roles are
   auto-deployable, so a shared pin would turn an automerged bump into a hand-merged one
   Renovate's per-depName grouping already bumps the three copies in one PR, and
   `test_the_image_pins_stay_in_lockstep` fails if they drift apart.

   Invoke at the container-list indent, e.g.:

       {{ exportarr('sonarr', sonarr_k8s_exportarr_image, container_item.port,
                    additional_metrics=true) }}
#}
{% macro exportarr(app, image, app_port, metrics_port=9707, additional_metrics=false) -%}
- name: exportarr
          image: {{ image }}
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          # The subcommand selects the API dialect — sonarr/radarr/prowlarr speak different
          # versions (v3/v3/v1) and expose different collectors.
          #
          # `additional_metrics` is a PER-APP parameter, not a blanket arg, because the flag
          # only buys something on sonarr. It gates sonarr's episode collector — the
          # `sonarr_episode_monitored_total` / `_unmonitored_total` / `_quality_total`
          # series a quality-mix chart needs — and upstream annotates it `(slow)`: the
          # collector then issues two extra app API calls (`episodefile`, `episode`) per
          # series per scrape. radarr's collector has no such block and prowlarr's has none
          # either, so passing it there would cost a flag's worth of drift for no series.
          args: ["{{ app }}"{% if additional_metrics %}, "--enable-additional-metrics"{% endif %}]
          ports:
            - name: metrics
              containerPort: {{ metrics_port }}
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            runAsNonRoot: true
            runAsUser: {{ puid }}
            runAsGroup: {{ pgid }}
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
          env:
            - name: PORT
              value: "{{ metrics_port }}"
            # localhost, not the Service name: same pod, same network namespace. This is also
            # what keeps the sidecar out of the *arr's NetworkPolicy caller list — traffic that
            # never leaves the pod is not ingress.
            - name: URL
              value: "http://localhost:{{ app_port }}"
            # secretKeyRef, not a literal: the pod spec then carries a reference, so
            # `kubectl get pod -o yaml` and `describe pod` never show the key.
            - name: APIKEY
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: {{ app }}-exportarr
                  key: api_key
            - name: TZ
              value: "{{ tz }}"
            # At info it logs one line per API request of every scrape.
            - name: LOG_LEVEL
              value: warn
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /healthz
              port: {{ metrics_port }}
            initialDelaySeconds: 30
            periodSeconds: 30
          # A readinessProbe would be actively harmful here. The *arr and this sidecar share one
          # pod, so a not-ready sidecar takes the whole pod out of its Service — an exporter
          # outage would become an application outage, which inverts the point of monitoring.
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 5m
              memory: 24Mi
            limits:
              # Without an explicit cpu limit the namespace LimitRange default applies, and
              # that default is `cpu: "2"` (k8s/vars.yml) -- 400x this container's own
              # request.
              #
              # DECIDED: size this cap against the CFS quota per PERIOD, never against the
              # average rate. The average is misleading here: the sidecar idles between scrapes
              # and does all of its work in one burst every 60s, so a 24h mean of ~0.5
              # millicores hides a burst that a per-period quota throttles. 100m is 10ms of
              # CPU per 100ms period and sonarr's scrape costs 25-86ms, so it spills across 4-7
              # periods and is throttled. 500m is 50ms per period, so most sonarr scrapes fit in
              # one. One shared value keeps the three from drifting; it is a limit, so an idle
              # sidecar costs nothing more.
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 64Mi
{%- endmacro %}
